Resending to include LKML, since I accidentally send this first just to
Ingo...

On Sun, 27 Jan 2008 23:14:22 +0100, Ingo Molnar inscribed:
> * Benjamin K. Stuhl <benjamin.stuhl@colorado.edu> wrote:
> >   I recently upgraded from 2.6.23.x to 2.6.24 and found that
> > whereas with 2.6.23.x I could have multiple "make -j3" compilation
> > runs going in the background with no real problems (on a dual-core
> > Core 2), with 2.6.24 I get multiple-second lags when trying to
> > switch tasks in KDE.
>=20
> that sounds bad ... Could you send me the output of:
>=20
>   http://people.redhat.com/mingo/cfs-scheduler/tools/cfs-debug-info.sh
>=20
> while such delays are happening? Please enable CONFIG_SCHED_DEBUG=3Dy
> and CONFIG_SCHEDSTATS=3Dy as well before doing the dump. Also please
> send your .config.

Ok, I've privately sent Ingo the results of cfs-debug-info.sh and
appended my .config. Also, one other important point which I'm
embarrassed to have forgotten to mention: this is with the nvidia
module loaded. I can't obviously reproduce it without the nvidia
module, but I also can't run a compositing KDE desktop -- the test case
-- without it. Rather a catch-22. :-( (Which is why I forgot to mention
it: the test case of window-flipping under KDE required it and so I
forgot that using it is considered unusual.) So it could be the nvidia
driver's known usage of sched_yield() or something else pathological,
or it could just be something about having a composited environment?

I have tried running with 4 subtracted
from /proc/sys/kernel/sched_features (making it now 35, it defaulted
to 39) and also with /proc/sys/kernel/sched_latency_ns reduced to
1000000; neither helped. Echoing 1 to
/proc/sys/kernel/sched_compat_yield didn't help either. (Suggested
by Ingo in a response to the private email.)

Thanks, and apologies for forgetting about the nvidia module,
-- BKS

